-- STATE-AS-LEDGER x T6: ledger effects inside a thunk force poison the
-- WHNFWhen (temporal-fingerprint) cache — the force is cached as a plain
-- WHNF (snapshot at first force) instead.
--
-- Without the poison, a shared thunk that BOTH writes the ledger and reads
-- the temporal context was cached as WHNFWhen; using it again under a
-- different temporal context RE-FORCED it, so the RECORD fired AGAIN (a
-- double-write: two `RECORD `x`` rows in the directive ledger below), and a
-- RECALL in such a thunk could observe a different ledger on each use of the
-- one shared binding.
--
-- Everything here is INTRA-directive on purpose: cross-directive shapes are
-- masked by the per-directive heap rebuild ('forEachDirectiveFreshHeap').
--
-- The test harness pins the clock: JL4_FIXED_NOW = 2025-01-31T15:45:30Z.

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Write side: the RECORD fires exactly once per first force
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`stamped write` MEANS (RECORD `x` IS 100) PLUS DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: 0 (one shared binding, one first-force value used twice) and
-- exactly ONE `RECORD `x`` ledger row.
-- (un-poisoned: -1857 — override value minus a re-forced ambient value —
-- and TWO ledger rows)
#EVAL (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `stamped write`) MINUS `stamped write`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Read side: one shared binding observes ONE ledger snapshot
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`recall or today` MEANS
  CONSIDER RECALL `x`
  WHEN JUST v THEN v
  WHEN NOTHING THEN DATE_SERIAL TODAY

-- expected: LIST 737789, 7, 737789 — both uses of the shared binding see the
-- FIRST force's ledger snapshot (cell `x` unwritten, so the override serial
-- 737789 = DATE_SERIAL of 2020-01-01), with the explicit RECORD in between.
-- (un-poisoned: LIST 737789, 7, 7 — the third use re-forced under the
-- ambient context and read the meanwhile-written 7: one binding, two values)
#EVAL LIST (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `recall or today`), (RECORD `x` IS 7), `recall or today`

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Control: no temporal read — a ledger thunk already snapshots this way
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`recall or zero` MEANS
  CONSIDER RECALL `x`
  WHEN JUST v THEN v
  WHEN NOTHING THEN 0

-- expected: LIST 0, 7, 0 (identical shape to the poisoned case above —
-- the poison makes ledger thunks with temporal reads behave like ledger
-- thunks without them, and guards against a never-cache "fix" which would
-- yield LIST 0, 7, 7 here)
#EVAL LIST (`EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 2020)) `recall or zero`), (RECORD `x` IS 7), `recall or zero`
